BIOFARM AI — Robust Viability of Ecologically Constrained Bio-Industrial Systems: Processing Capacity as a State-Dependent Constraint on Admissible Biological Throughput

Ouadi Maakoul — SI TEAM — BIOFARM AI — 15 November 2026

---

Abstract

Integrated livestock–crop–processing systems are conventionally modelled by treating processing capacity as an optimisation variable derived from production targets, or as an economic constraint on farm management. This paper inverts that logic. An Ecologically Constrained Bio-Industrial System (ECBIS) is defined as a coupled dynamical system in which ecological and animal-welfare feasibility conditions constitute the admissible state space, and industrial processing capacity is formally subordinated to admissible biological throughput through a state-dependent constraint. The central analytical object is the robust viability kernel of the coupled system under bounded uncertainty, parameterised by installed capacity. Three propositions are stated and made falsifiable: a capacity-overhang proposition, in which an explicit minimum-throughput requirement together with a costly closure option transmits industrial pressure into ecological constraint erosion; a composition-shift proposition, which predicts a directional reordering of herd robustness rankings under coupling through an offtake-variability channel; and an authority-boundedness proposition, decomposed into shield efficacy under measurement noise and human–AI override dynamics. The framework is distinguished from fisheries viability analysis and from Hamilton–Jacobi robust viability for waste-to-energy systems by three features that have no counterpart in those literatures: endogenous biological reproduction with age structure, non-compensatory welfare constraints, and a financial transmission mechanism in which capacity overhang resolves either through ecological violation or through exit. A computational protocol based on inner-approximated viability kernels, calibrated to the Moroccan national herd, is specified.

Keywords: viability theory; robust viability kernel; ecological constraints; mixed livestock systems; processing capacity overhang; bounded uncertainty; shielded AI; Hamilton–Jacobi reachability.

