1. Introduction

1.1 The coupling problem

The design of integrated livestock–crop–processing systems is typically framed as an optimisation: given a production target, choose land allocation, herd composition, and processing capacity to minimise cost or environmental burden. This framing treats processing capacity as a free design variable. In operational systems, the causal direction is frequently reversed. Once processing infrastructure is installed, high fixed costs and minimum efficient scale create pressure to maintain throughput. That pressure is transmitted to the biological subsystem as an incentive to remove animals at a rate that may not be ecologically admissible in all years.

The empirical record supports this mechanism. In the United States beef-packing sector, installed capacity has exceeded available cattle supply for several years: packers have been “routinely losing $200–$400 per head on every head slaughtered and fabricated since 2023,” and capacity has been reduced through plant closures and shift reductions rather than through a restoration of ecological balance. The resolution pathway matters for the present framework: over-capacity can resolve through exit, through sustained financial loss, or through intensified biological extraction. Which pathway dominates is a design question, not an inevitability, and it is the question this paper formalises.

The question addressed here is precise: under what conditions can a processing subsystem be coupled to an ecologically constrained livestock subsystem without the coupling shrinking the set of ecologically admissible trajectories? The question is not whether coupling is profitable, nor whether it is desirable in any broader sense. It is whether coupling can be rendered viability-preserving, and how to characterise the configurations in which it is not.

1.2 Relation to antecedent work

Viability theory has been applied to bio-economic fisheries by Béné, Doyen and Gabay, who used the viability kernel to identify “overexploitation” configurations and “irreversible overexploitation configurations related to resource extinction”. VIKAASA provides computational approximations for low-dimensional viability kernels and has been applied to by-catch fisheries. These antecedents establish that processing or harvesting capacity interacting with biological constraints can shrink the viable set; the capacity-overhang mechanism is therefore not novel in itself.

A closer precedent is the Hamilton–Jacobi formulation of robust viability for waste-to-energy systems under adversarial inflow uncertainty. That framework characterises the viability kernel as the zero-sublevel set of a value function satisfying a constrained Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman equation, with waste inflow modelled as an adversarial disturbance in a zero-sum differential game, and demonstrates that increasing inflow uncertainty significantly reduces the viability domain. Its state includes a processing-capacity component with investment and depreciation dynamics, and its uncertainty treatment is explicitly adversarial.

The ECBIS framework differs from both antecedents in three structural respects. First, the biological subsystem reproduces endogenously and carries age structure, so admissible offtake is not a function of standing biomass alone but of reproductive replacement capacity. Second, admissibility includes non-compensatory welfare constraints that cannot be traded against economic return. Third, the financial transmission mechanism is modelled explicitly, with a costly closure option, so that over-capacity can resolve through exit rather than only through ecological violation. These differences are not decorative: they generate the composition-shift and authority-boundedness propositions, which have no counterpart in fisheries or waste-to-energy viability analysis.

1.3 Contribution and falsifiability

The contribution is a formal framework and a set of propositions whose rejection conditions are specified in advance. The framework does not claim empirical validation. It claims sufficient precision that its propositions can be rejected in computational experiments. Section 2 defines the system and the admissibility structure. Section 3 develops the robust viability formalism with an explicit financial transmission mechanism. Section 4 states the propositions. Section 5 positions the framework against the antecedent literatures. Section 6 specifies the numerical protocol. Section 7 treats the AI layer as a shielded component. Section 8 states the limitations.

