2. System definition and admissibility

2.1 State space

Let t \in \{0,1,\dots,T\} index discrete periods at monthly or seasonal resolution. The coupled state is

x_t = (E_t, L_t, I_t, F_t) \in \mathcal{X} \subset \mathbb{R}^{n_x},

where:

· E_t = (S_t, W_t, V_t, N_t) is the ecological state: soil condition, water availability and quality, vegetation and habitat state, and nutrient balance.
· L_t = (n_{1,t},\dots,n_{k,t}, A_t, H_t) is the biological state: population sizes n_{i,t} for k animal categories (species × age class), an age-structure vector A_t (e.g., proportion of breeding females), and a vector H_t of health and welfare indicators.
· I_t = (Q_t, C_t, K_t) is the industrial state: cumulative processed throughput, operational capacity, and product inventory. Installed capacity \bar{C} is a parameter; operational capacity C_t = s_t \bar{C} is a state component, where s_t \in \{0,1\} is an activation decision.
· F_t = (R_t, D_t, B_t) is the financial state: reserves, debt obligations, and disposable budget.

The dimension n_x is at least 4 + k + 3 + 3. Biodiversity and soil organic carbon are not continuously measurable; they are treated as periodically observed state components with measurement uncertainty, not as real-time control variables.

2.2 Controls, disturbances, and the admissibility structure

Controls are u_t \in \mathcal{U}(x_t): grazing allocation, supplementation, reproduction and culling decisions, water allocation, activation decision s_t, and processing throughput q_t^{\mathrm{proc}}. Controls are feedback mappings u_t = \mathfrak{u}(t,x_t), not open-loop sequences chosen after observing the disturbance realisation.

Disturbances are \xi_t \in \Xi \subset \mathbb{R}^{n_\xi}: precipitation deviation, forage yield deviation, energy-price deviation, disease incidence, and market-demand fluctuation. The uncertainty set \Xi is compact.

Admissibility is defined by three separate objects, not a single set:

\mathcal{K}_X \subseteq \mathcal{X} \quad \text{(state constraints)},

\mathcal{U}(x) \subseteq \mathcal{U} \quad \text{(control constraints for each } x \in \mathcal{X}\text{)},

\Xi \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{n_\xi} \quad \text{(disturbance set, defining the adversary)}.

The state-constraint set is the intersection of four non-compensatory families:

\mathcal{K}_X = \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{eco}} \cap \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{welfare}} \cap \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{sanitary}} \cap \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{resource}}.

No element of \mathcal{K}_X can be traded against another. A configuration that violates a welfare lower bound is inadmissible regardless of economic return. This is the formal expression of the non-compensation principle and distinguishes the framework from multi-objective optimisation in which all dimensions are commensurable.

Epistemic status of constraints. Each constraint family is assigned an explicit observation status. Water volume in an instrumented reservoir and processing throughput are treated as deterministically observable. Soil condition, vegetation state, and welfare indicators are treated as periodically observed with bounded measurement error. For the latter, constraints are enforced on a conservative quantile of the posterior estimate, not on the point estimate:

\Pr\bigl(h_j(x_t) \ge 0 \mid \hat{x}_t\bigr) \ge 1 - \alpha_j, \quad j \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{welfare}},

with \alpha_j pre-registered. This ensures that the admissible set is well-defined under measurement noise and that the non-compensation principle is not silently relaxed when welfare is estimated rather than measured.

2.3 The throughput constraint and operational capacity

The structural novelty of the ECBIS formulation is the asymmetric treatment of capacity. Installed capacity \bar{C} determines what the industrial subsystem can process. The ecological throughput constraint determines what it may process:

q_t^{\mathrm{proc}} \le \min\{C_t, \Phi(E_t, L_t)\},

where C_t = s_t \bar{C}.

The industrial subsystem does not appear on the right-hand side of the ecological constraint. This asymmetry is the formal core of the ecological subordination principle.

2.4 Constructive definition of \Phi

The admissible throughput function \Phi is not assumed in closed form. It is defined constructively as the solution of an auxiliary finite-horizon viability problem:

\Phi(x_t) = \max \Bigl\{ q \in \mathbb{R}_+ : \exists (u_\tau)_{\tau=t}^{t+T_h-1} \text{ with } q_t^{\mathrm{proc}} = q, \; \forall \tau,\; x_\tau \in \mathcal{K}_X,\; x_{\tau+1} = f(x_\tau,u_\tau,\bar{\xi}) \Bigr\},

where T_h is a pre-registered planning horizon (three to five years for livestock systems, reflecting reproductive and forage-regeneration cycles) and \bar{\xi} is the nominal disturbance trajectory. \Phi is therefore derived from f and \mathcal{K}_X, not invented. Its calibration requires forage-growth parameters, reproductive-rate parameters by species and age class, and welfare-threshold parameters. Three reduced-form regimes are pre-registered for sensitivity analysis:

1. Replacement-limited regime: \Phi is determined by the steady-state reproductive replacement rate, \Phi \approx \rho \sum_i \nu_i n_{i,t}^{\mathrm{breed}}, where \rho is a safe offtake fraction and \nu_i is the reproductive rate.
2. Drought-emergency regime: \Phi is determined by the maximum offtake consistent with avoiding forced liquidation in a drought year, \Phi = \max\{q : \text{post-offtake herd survives } \xi_{\mathrm{drought}}\}.
3. Welfare-density regime: \Phi is determined by the maximum stocking density compatible with welfare lower bounds, \Phi = \max\{q : H_t \ge \underline{H} \text{ after offtake}\}.

Proposition 1 is tested under all three regimes. If the capacity-overhang effect is present only under one regime, that is the empirical content of the proposition, and it must be reported as such.

