3. Robust viability with explicit transmission mechanisms

3.1 Deterministic viability kernel

For a given feedback control \mathfrak{u} and a disturbance trajectory, the deterministic viability kernel is

\mathrm{Viab}_{\mathcal{K}_X}(\mathfrak{u}) = \bigl\{ x_0 \in \mathcal{K}_X : \forall t,\; x_t \in \mathcal{K}_X,\; \mathfrak{u}(t,x_t) \in \mathcal{U}(x_t),\; x_{t+1} = f(x_t,\mathfrak{u}(t,x_t),\xi_t) \bigr\}.

The kernel is the union over admissible feedbacks:

\mathrm{Viab}_{\mathcal{K}_X} = \bigcup_{\mathfrak{u}} \mathrm{Viab}_{\mathcal{K}_X}(\mathfrak{u}).

3.2 Robust and stochastic kernels

Under bounded uncertainty, the robust viability kernel is

\mathcal{V}^{\Xi} = \bigl\{ x_0 \in \mathcal{K}_X : \exists \mathfrak{u},\; \forall (\xi_t)_{t=0}^{T-1} \in \Xi^T,\; \forall t,\; x_t \in \mathcal{K}_X,\; \mathfrak{u}(t,x_t) \in \mathcal{U}(x_t),\; x_{t+1} = f(x_t,\mathfrak{u}(t,x_t),\xi_t) \bigr\}.

This is a worst-case kernel. Because an adversarial \Xi that includes compound drought, energy-price spikes, and disease may produce an empty kernel, a stochastic viability arm is computed alongside it. For \beta \in (0,1), the stochastic kernel is

\mathcal{V}^{\beta} = \bigl\{ x_0 \in \mathcal{K}_X : \exists \mathfrak{u},\; \Pr\bigl(\forall t,\; x_t \in \mathcal{K}_X \mid x_0,\mathfrak{u}\bigr) \ge \beta \bigr\},

with \beta pre-registered at 0.90 and 0.95. Reporting both \mathcal{V}^{\Xi} and \mathcal{V}^{\beta} separates the price of worst-case robustness from the effect of the coupling constraint itself. A framework that reports only the worst-case kernel cannot distinguish an empty kernel caused by an over-wide \Xi from one caused by genuine infeasibility.

3.3 Financial transmission mechanism

The coupling constraint q_t^{\mathrm{proc}} \le \min\{C_t,\Phi\} is an upper bound only. An upper bound cannot shrink a viability kernel if the controller is free to process zero. The transmission mechanism must be specified explicitly, and it is specified here in the form of a minimum-throughput requirement together with a costly activation decision.

Operational capacity is C_t = s_t \bar{C} with s_t \in \{0,1\}. When active (s_t = 1), the plant incurs a fixed operating cost d_1 and is subject to a minimum-throughput constraint:

q_t^{\mathrm{proc}} \ge \alpha C_t, \quad \alpha \in (0,1),

where \alpha is the minimum load factor required to cover fixed costs. When mothballed (s_t = 0), the plant incurs a mothballing cost d_0 < d_1 and q_t^{\mathrm{proc}} = 0. Switching from active to mothballed incurs a closure cost c_{\mathrm{close}}; switching from mothballed to active incurs a restart cost c_{\mathrm{restart}}.

The financial dynamics are

R_{t+1} = R_t + p_t q_t^{\mathrm{proc}} - c(x_t,u_t) - d(s_t) - c_{\mathrm{switch}}(s_t,s_{t-1}) - D_t,

where p_t is the output price, c(x_t,u_t) is the variable production cost, and D_t is debt service. If R_t < \underline{R} < 0, the system is in default and a forced-liquidation control is applied; forced liquidation is itself a control that may violate ecological constraints and is therefore outside \mathcal{K}_X, which makes default an absorbing inadmissible state.

The mechanism is now explicit: when \bar{C} exceeds \Phi(x_t), an active plant faces \alpha \bar{C} > \Phi(x_t). The only admissible active controls satisfy q_t^{\mathrm{proc}} \in [\alpha\bar{C}, \Phi(x_t)], which is empty. The manager must either (i) mothball the plant at cost c_{\mathrm{close}}, (ii) operate below the minimum load and absorb losses until reserves are exhausted, or (iii) violate the ecological constraint by removing animals above \Phi, which moves the state outside \mathcal{K}_X. Pathway (iii) is the ecological violation pathway. Pathway (i) is the exit pathway. Which dominates is determined by c_{\mathrm{close}}, \alpha, and the initial reserve R_0.

3.4 The capacity-parameterised kernel

For fixed installed capacity \bar{C}, let \mathcal{V}(\bar{C}) denote the robust viability kernel of the coupled system with the financial transmission mechanism of Section 3.3. The object of central interest is the mapping

\bar{C} \longmapsto \mathcal{V}(\bar{C}).

The capacity-overhang hypothesis is that \mathcal{V}(\bar{C}) is non-increasing beyond a threshold \bar{C}^*, where \bar{C}^* is the largest installed capacity for which there exists an admissible active operating point for all x \in \mathcal{V}(\bar{C}^*) and all \xi \in \Xi. Formally,

\bar{C}^* = \sup \bigl\{ \bar{C} : \forall x \in \mathcal{V}(\bar{C}),\; \exists q \in [\alpha\bar{C}, \Phi(x)] \bigr\}.

For \bar{C} > \bar{C}^*, no active operating point is admissible on the kernel, and the only admissible industrial state is s_t = 0. The kernel then shrinks to the subset of states from which mothballing is financially survivable:

\mathcal{V}(\bar{C}) \subseteq \bigl\{ x \in \mathcal{V}(\bar{C}^*) : R_t \ge \underline{R} + c_{\mathrm{close}} \bigr\}.

This is a strict inclusion whenever c_{\mathrm{close}} > 0 and the reserve distribution has mass below the threshold.

