4. Propositions

Proposition 1 — Capacity overhang with explicit transmission

There exist thresholds \bar{C}^*, \alpha^*, and c_{\mathrm{close}}^* such that for \bar{C} > \bar{C}^*, \alpha > \alpha^*, and c_{\mathrm{close}} < c_{\mathrm{close}}^*, the robust viability kernel satisfies

\mathcal{V}(\bar{C}) \subsetneq \mathcal{V}(\bar{C}^*).

The mechanism is the empty active-operating interval: \alpha\bar{C} > \Phi(x) on a set of states of positive measure in \mathcal{V}(\bar{C}^*), and the closure cost is low enough that mothballing is financially survivable only from a strict subset of the kernel. The proposition does not assert that over-capacity always causes ecological violation; it asserts that when closure is not a viable escape for a subset of states, the coupling transmits industrial pressure into ecological constraint erosion or financial default.

Falsification condition. If \mathcal{V}(\bar{C}) is invariant to \bar{C} for all \bar{C} in the tested range, or if the kernel is non-empty and invariant because closure is always available at zero cost, Proposition 1 is rejected for that parameterisation. The experiment must report the closure-cost sweep explicitly; a null result at high c_{\mathrm{close}} is not evidence against the proposition, it is evidence that the exit pathway dominates.

Proposition 2 — Directional composition shift under coupling

Under coupling, the robustness ranking of herd compositions shifts away from species with high reproductive variance and low adult survival, and toward species with high steady-state replacement capacity and high adult buffering capacity. The mechanism is the offtake-variability channel: the coupling constraint penalises variability in admissible offtake more heavily than it penalises a lower mean offtake, because the minimum-throughput requirement \alpha\bar{C} binds in low-\Phi states. Species whose \Phi fluctuates widely across disturbance scenarios are therefore disadvantaged relative to species whose \Phi is stable, even if the latter have a lower mean \Phi.

Metrics. Two composition metrics are pre-registered: (i) normalised kernel volume \kappa(\bar{C}) = |\mathcal{V}(\bar{C})| / |\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{adm}}|, and (ii) the minimum distance to the kernel boundary for a fixed reference herd composition,

m(\bar{C}) = \min_{x \in \partial\mathcal{V}(\bar{C})} \|x - x^{\mathrm{ref}}\|.

If the two metrics rank compositions differently, both rankings are reported. The volume metric is computed on a grid whose resolution is pre-registered; a coarse-grid ranking that reverses on a finer grid is reported as non-robust.

Falsification condition. If the robustness-maximising composition is identical with and without the coupling constraint, or if the ranking changes only because the economic constraint changed from M0 to M1 and not because of the coupling from M2 to M3, Proposition 2 is rejected. The M2 \to M3 comparison holds economics fixed.

Proposition 3a — Shield efficacy under measurement noise

Let \hat{x}_t be the state estimate with bounded error \|\hat{x}_t - x_t\| \le \epsilon. Let the verification layer admit a proposed control \hat{u}_t only if (\hat{x}_t,\hat{u}_t) \in \mathcal{K}_X and \hat{x}_{t+1} \in \mathcal{V}_\epsilon(\bar{C}), where

\mathcal{V}_\epsilon(\bar{C}) = \bigl\{ x \in \mathcal{V}(\bar{C}) : \operatorname{dist}(x,\partial\mathcal{V}(\bar{C})) > \kappa(\epsilon) \bigr\}

is a shrunk kernel with margin \kappa(\epsilon) chosen to cover the estimation error. If the fallback rule is itself a T_h-period viable recovery plan from every state in \mathcal{V}_\epsilon(\bar{C}), then the shielded AI has violation frequency no greater than the unshielded AI under disturbances \Xi and noise bound \epsilon.

Falsification condition. If the shielded system exhibits a higher violation frequency than the unshielded system at some \epsilon > 0, the containment argument fails. The experiment must test \epsilon > 0, not \epsilon = 0; a shield that is sound only at zero noise is a theorem about an idealised system.

Proposition 3b — Human–AI system with override authority

Let the human operator override the AI proposal with probability p_{\mathrm{ov}} when the proposal deviates from the operator's own heuristic by more than a threshold \delta, and accept the AI proposal otherwise. If p_{\mathrm{ov}} and \delta are pre-registered and the human-alone baseline operates without access to the kernel, then the human–AI system has violation frequency no greater than the human-alone system whenever the AI proposal is drawn from \mathcal{V}_\epsilon(\bar{C}) and the override does not move the state outside \mathcal{K}_X.

Falsification condition. If the human–AI system violates more frequently than the human-alone baseline at the pre-registered (p_{\mathrm{ov}},\delta), the proposition is rejected. The experiment must not fit p_{\mathrm{ov}} or \delta after observing outcomes; both are fixed in the analysis plan.

Proposition 4 — Non-vacuity of the robust kernel

There exists a non-empty uncertainty set \Xi^* \subseteq \Xi such that \mathcal{V}^{\Xi^*}(\bar{C}) is non-empty and the capacity-overhang effect of Proposition 1 is detectable. The numerical protocol reports \kappa(\bar{C}) as a joint function of \bar{C} and the diameter of \Xi, and identifies the maximum disturbance amplitude at which the robust kernel collapses. If the kernel is empty for all \bar{C} at every \Xi of operational interest, the framework is not applicable to that system, and this is reported as a scope condition rather than as a failed experiment.

