5. Positioning against antecedent frameworks

The framework inherits from three literatures and must be distinguished from each with precision. The following table states, for each claim, the closest prior art and the specific addition. A claim with no entry in the third column is deleted.

Claim Closest prior art ECBIS addition
Capacity pressure erodes biological viability Béné–Doyen–Gabay viability analysis of fisheries overexploitation; robust HJB viability for waste-to-energy systems under adversarial uncertainty Financial transmission through minimum-throughput and costly closure in a system with endogenous reproduction and age structure; the exit pathway as an explicit competing resolution to ecological violation.
Uncertainty shrinks the viability kernel Robust farming-systems viability; WtE HJB formulation Separation of worst-case and stochastic kernels; non-vacuity as a pre-registered design condition rather than a post hoc caveat.
Composition affects robustness Puech et al. on mixed ruminant herds Directional prediction: coupling penalises offtake variability, shifting ranking toward high-buffering species; two pre-registered metrics.
AI authority containment Simplex architecture and runtime assurance: advanced controller with verified fallback and verified switching logic Belief-state kernel with measurement-noise margin; biological-inertia fallback as a multi-period recovery plan; welfare constraints inside the verified set.
Endogenous ecological regeneration constrains offtake Forage-growth and carrying-capacity models \Phi defined constructively as the solution of an auxiliary viability problem, not as a fixed carrying-capacity fraction.

The distinguishing move is Proposition 2 combined with welfare constraints and endogenous ecology. Proposition 1 alone competes with a well-established literature on capacity and viability. Proposition 2 has no direct antecedent: waste-to-energy systems have no herd composition, and fisheries viability analysis has not treated species composition as a robustness-ranking problem under a processing-capacity constraint with a closure option. The novelty centre of the paper is therefore Proposition 2, supported by Propositions 1, 3a, 3b, and 4.

