6. Computational protocol

6.1 Model comparison

Five models are implemented on a common state–control–disturbance specification, differing in constraint sets and decision logic.

Model Constraint set Decision logic Purpose
M0 Economic only Static profit maximisation Unconstrained baseline
M1 Economic + ecological Static optimisation within \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{eco}} Effect of ecological constraints
M2 Ecological + welfare, dynamic Receding-horizon feedback within \mathcal{K}_X Effect of time and biological dynamics
M3 M2 + industrial coupling with minimum-throughput and closure Receding-horizon feedback within \mathcal{K}_X and the financial transmission mechanism Effect of coupling
M4 M3 + bounded uncertainty Robust feedback within \mathcal{V}^{\Xi}(\bar{C}), with stochastic arm \mathcal{V}^{\beta} Effect of robustness requirement

The comparison M2 \to M3 isolates coupling. M3 \to M4 isolates the price of robustness. M0 \to M1 isolates the cost of ecological constraints. Each comparison holds all other components fixed.

6.2 Computation method

Viability kernels are computed by Hamilton–Jacobi level-set reachability for a reduced four-dimensional state:

\tilde{x}_t = (\tilde{E}_t, L^{\mathrm{cattle}}_t, L^{\mathrm{small\ ruminant}}_t, R_t),

where \tilde{E}_t aggregates soil and water into a single ecological index, and the biological state is aggregated into two groups. The kernel is the zero-sublevel set of a value function satisfying a constrained Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman equation, solved with a Local Lax–Friedrichs scheme on a grid whose resolution is pre-registered. This follows the method demonstrated for robust viability of waste-to-energy systems.

For the higher-dimensional full state, the grid method is not used. Instead, the protocol reports an inner approximation \underline{\mathcal{V}}(\bar{C}) \subseteq \mathcal{V}(\bar{C}) computed by a Lagrangian or reachable-set under-approximation, together with a certified outer approximation \overline{\mathcal{V}}(\bar{C}). Any kernel volume reported is a lower bound. The relationship between the reduced-state kernel and the full-state bounds is reported explicitly; the reduced-state kernel is not described as an approximation of the full kernel.

Computation cost is reported for every \bar{C} level: grid size, wall-clock time, and a convergence check comparing the kernel at grid spacing h and h/2. If the capacity sweep is computationally infeasible at the planned resolution, the number of \bar{C} levels is reduced and the number of disturbance replicates is increased, not the reverse.

6.3 Disturbance scenarios and uncertainty calibration

The uncertainty set \Xi is calibrated to historical variability at the calibration site rather than defined adversarially without bounds. For a Moroccan calibration, the relevant variability is the multi-year drought cycle: the national cattle and sheep herd declined by 38% between 2016 and 2025, with cattle and camel numbers falling by about 30% relative to average levels, and rainfall substantially below the 30-year mean. The uncertainty set includes precipitation deviation calibrated to the observed drought distribution, forage-yield deviation implied by the precipitation deviation, energy-price deviation calibrated to observed feed and fuel price series, and a binary disease-incidence process.

Four scenario families are tested: nominal (no deviation from the mean); single-shock drought in a randomly selected period; compound drought followed by an energy-price spike; and worst-case trajectory chosen adversarially within \Xi. The robust kernel is computed against the worst-case family; the stochastic kernel is computed against the empirical distribution. The non-vacuity frontier is reported: the maximum disturbance amplitude at which \mathcal{V}^{\Xi}(\bar{C}) becomes empty, for each \bar{C}.

6.4 Capacity sweep and closure sweep

For M3 and M4, installed capacity \bar{C} is varied from below the minimum admissible throughput to well above the maximum admissible throughput over \Xi. The kernel volume \kappa(\bar{C}) is computed at each level. Proposition 1 predicts non-increase beyond \bar{C}^*. The closure cost c_{\mathrm{close}} and the minimum-load factor \alpha are swept jointly with \bar{C}, because Proposition 1 is conditional on \alpha > \alpha^* and c_{\mathrm{close}} < c_{\mathrm{close}}^*. A capacity sweep without a closure sweep cannot distinguish the ecological-violation pathway from the exit pathway.

6.5 Metrics

The primary metric is the normalised kernel volume

\kappa(\bar{C}) = \frac{|\mathcal{V}(\bar{C})|}{|\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{adm}}|},

where \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{adm}} is a bounded reference domain whose definition and discretisation are pre-registered.

Secondary metrics are: the capacity overhang ratio \bar{C} / \sup_{x \in \mathcal{V}(\bar{C})} \Phi(x); violation frequency, the proportion of simulated periods in which the realised state exits \mathcal{K}_X; recovery cost, the additional financial expenditure required to return to \mathcal{V}(\bar{C}) after a disturbance; and the composition robustness margin m(\bar{C}) defined in Proposition 2. All metrics are reported for both the worst-case and stochastic arms.

6.6 Calibration site

The reference calibration is the Moroccan national herd: 23.1 million sheep, 7.47 million goats, 2.09 million cattle, and 106,044 camels, recorded in the 2025 national census. The drought disturbance is calibrated to the observed 2016–2025 cycle. If the calibration is not completed, the experiment is labelled synthetic, and no claim of local parameterisation is made.

