Appendix B — Formal derivations

B.1 Constructive definition of the admissible throughput function

For x_t \in \mathcal{K}_X, define

\Phi(x_t) \;=\; \max\Bigl\{\, q \in \mathbb{R}_+ \;:\; \exists (u_\tau)_{\tau=t}^{t+T_h-1},\; u_\tau \in \mathcal{U}(x_\tau),\;\; q_t^{\mathrm{proc}} = q,\;\; \forall \tau \in [t,t+T_h),\;\; x_\tau \in \mathcal{K}_X \Bigr\},

with x_{\tau+1} = f(x_\tau,u_\tau,\bar{\xi}) under the nominal disturbance trajectory. The following properties are used in the propositions and are stated as assumptions, not derived.

Assumption B.1 (Well-posedness). For each x \in \mathcal{K}_X, the set over which the maximum is taken is non-empty and compact, so \Phi(x) is attained.

Assumption B.2 (Regularity). \Phi is upper semi-continuous on \mathcal{K}_X and Lipschitz on compact subsets with constant L_\Phi.

Assumption B.3 (Monotonicity in age structure). For fixed total biomass, \Phi is non-decreasing in the proportion of breeding females A_t. This excludes the degenerate case in which a herd of identical biomass but different age structure has identical admissible offtake, and it is the formal content of the claim that the biological state is not reducible to a scalar stock.

Under Assumptions B.1–B.3, \Phi is a derived object: it is the value of an auxiliary viability problem, not an assumed carrying-capacity fraction. The three reduced-form regimes of Section 2.4 are approximations to this object and are used only for sensitivity analysis; they are not substituted for it in the primary computation.

B.2 Proposition 1 — monotonicity and strict inclusion

Write the active-plant feasible throughput interval as

\mathcal{Q}_{\bar{C}}(x) = \begin{cases} \bigl[\alpha\bar{C},\; \min\{\bar{C},\Phi(x)\}\bigr], & s=1, \\[4pt] \{0\}, & s=0. \end{cases}

Lemma B.4 (Nestedness of active feasible sets). For 0<\bar{C}_1<\bar{C}_2, if \mathcal{Q}_{\bar{C}_2}(x) \neq \varnothing then \mathcal{Q}_{\bar{C}_2}(x) \subseteq \mathcal{Q}_{\bar{C}_1}(x).

Proof. If \mathcal{Q}_{\bar{C}_2}(x)\neq\varnothing, then \alpha\bar{C}_2 \le \min\{\bar{C}_2,\Phi(x)\}, hence \alpha\bar{C}_1<\alpha\bar{C}_2\le\Phi(x), so the lower endpoint of \mathcal{Q}_{\bar{C}_1} lies below the lower endpoint of \mathcal{Q}_{\bar{C}_2}, and the upper endpoint of \mathcal{Q}_{\bar{C}_1} is \min\{\bar{C}_1,\Phi(x)\}\ge \min\{\bar{C}_1,\min\{\bar{C}_2,\Phi(x)\}\}. Since \bar{C}_1<\bar{C}_2, the interval inclusion follows. \square

Lemma B.5 (Pathwise domination). If a control-disturbance trajectory (u_t,\xi_t) is admissible and viable under \bar{C}_2, the same trajectory is admissible and viable under \bar{C}_1<\bar{C}_2.

Proof. By Lemma B.4, each control u_t feasible under \bar{C}_2 at x_t is feasible under \bar{C}_1 at x_t. The transition map f and the financial recursion do not depend on \bar{C} except through q_t^{\mathrm{proc}}, which is fixed along the trajectory. Hence the realised state sequence is identical and remains in \mathcal{K}_X. \square

Proposition B.6 (Monotone non-increase). For 0<\bar{C}_1<\bar{C}_2, \mathcal{V}(\bar{C}_2)\subseteq\mathcal{V}(\bar{C}_1).

Proof. Immediate from Lemma B.5: every initial state with a viable admissible trajectory under \bar{C}_2 has the same trajectory viable under \bar{C}_1. \square

Proposition B.7 (Strict inclusion under closure infeasibility). Let \bar{C}_1<\bar{C}_2 and define

\mathcal{S}(\bar{C}_2) = \bigl\{ x \in \mathcal{V}(\bar{C}_1) \;:\; \Phi(x) < \alpha\bar{C}_2 \;\text{ and }\; R(x) < \underline{R} + c_{\mathrm{close}} + d_0 \bigr\}.

If \mathcal{S}(\bar{C}_2) has positive measure in \mathcal{V}(\bar{C}_1) and if for each x\in\mathcal{S}(\bar{C}_2) there exists a viable trajectory under \bar{C}_1 with q_t^{\mathrm{proc}}\in[\alpha\bar{C}_1,\min\{\bar{C}_1,\Phi(x)\}], then \mathcal{V}(\bar{C}_2)\subsetneq\mathcal{V}(\bar{C}_1).

Proof. Take x_0\in\mathcal{S}(\bar{C}_2). Under \bar{C}_2, active operation requires q\ge\alpha\bar{C}_2>\Phi(x_0), which violates the ecological throughput constraint; so no active admissible control exists at x_0. Inactive operation requires R(x_0)\ge\underline{R}+c_{\mathrm{close}}+d_0 to survive closure and subsequent debt service; by construction of \mathcal{S} this fails. Hence no admissible control exists at x_0, so x_0\notin\mathcal{V}(\bar{C}_2). By hypothesis x_0\in\mathcal{V}(\bar{C}_1). Therefore \mathcal{V}(\bar{C}_2)\subsetneq\mathcal{V}(\bar{C}_1). \square

Proposition B.7 is the formal statement of Proposition 1. It makes the three pre-registered conditions explicit: a capacity range (\bar{C}_1,\bar{C}_2), a load factor \alpha, and a closure-cost regime such that the set \mathcal{S} is non-empty with positive measure. The capacity-overhang effect is therefore not a consequence of the coupling constraint alone; it is a consequence of the interaction between the minimum-throughput requirement and the infeasibility of the exit pathway for a subset of states. This is why the numerical protocol sweeps c_{\mathrm{close}} and \alpha jointly with \bar{C}.

B.3 Proposition 2 — the offtake-variability channel

Let the disturbance set be finite or discretised, \Xi=\{\xi^{(1)},\dots,\xi^{(M)}\}, with weights w_m. For a composition i, let \Phi_i(x;\xi^{(m)}) denote the admissible throughput under disturbance realisation m. Define the binding probability

p_i(\bar{C}) \;=\; \sum_{m=1}^{M} w_m \,\mathbf{1}\bigl\{\Phi_i(x;\xi^{(m)}) < \alpha\bar{C}\bigr\}.

A composition with p_i(\bar{C})=0 is never forced into the empty active-operating interval; a composition with p_i(\bar{C})>0 is forced into closure or into violation in a positive fraction of scenarios.

Proposition B.8 (Ordering reversal). There exist two compositions i,j and a capacity \bar{C} such that i has higher uncoupled kernel volume and higher mean admissible throughput, yet j has lower binding probability and therefore higher coupled robustness.

Proof by construction. Let composition i have \Phi_i with high mean and high variance across \Xi, and composition j have \Phi_j with lower mean and low variance:

\mathbb{E}\Phi_i > \mathbb{E}\Phi_j, \qquad \mathrm{Var}(\Phi_i) \gg \mathrm{Var}(\Phi_j).

Choose \alpha\bar{C} such that \alpha\bar{C} lies between the lower quantiles of \Phi_i and \Phi_j: \alpha\bar{C} > q_{0.1}(\Phi_i) but \alpha\bar{C} < q_{0.1}(\Phi_j). Then p_i(\bar{C})>0.1 and p_j(\bar{C})=0. Under the uncoupled constraint set, composition i dominates in kernel volume because its mean offtake is higher. Under coupling, composition j dominates because it is never forced into the empty interval. The ranking reverses. \square

The directional content of Proposition 2 is that species with low reproductive variance and high adult buffering are favoured under coupling, because their \Phi has low variance and their binding probability is low. Species with high reproductive variance and low adult survival have high \Phi variance and are penalised. This is the offtake-variability channel; it is a distinct mechanism from the forage-demand channel, under which high-forage-demand species would be penalised instead. The two channels can predict opposite rankings in some regimes, and the experiment must report which channel dominates.

B.4 Proposition 3a — shield soundness with measurement error

Assumption B.9 (Lipschitz dynamics). There exists L_x such that for all x,x'\in\mathcal{X}, all u\in\mathcal{U}, all \xi\in\Xi:

\|f(x,u,\xi)-f(x',u,\xi)\| \le L_x\|x-x'\|.

Assumption B.10 (Bounded estimation error). \|\hat{x}_t - x_t\| \le \epsilon for all t.

Lemma B.11 (Margin requirement). Define the shrunk kernel

\mathcal{V}_\epsilon(\bar{C}) = \bigl\{ x \in \mathcal{V}(\bar{C}) : \operatorname{dist}(x,\partial\mathcal{V}(\bar{C})) > (L_x+1)\epsilon \bigr\}.

If \hat{x}_t\in\mathcal{V}_\epsilon(\bar{C}) and (\hat{x}_t,u_t)\in\mathcal{K}_X, then x_{t+1}\in\mathcal{V}(\bar{C}).

Proof. By Assumption B.9, \|x_{t+1}-\hat{x}_{t+1}\|\le L_x\epsilon. If the verification layer admits u_t, it does so because \hat{x}_{t+1}\in\mathcal{V}_\epsilon(\bar{C}); hence \operatorname{dist}(\hat{x}_{t+1},\partial\mathcal{V}(\bar{C}))>(L_x+1)\epsilon. Therefore

\operatorname{dist}(x_{t+1},\partial\mathcal{V}(\bar{C})) \ge \operatorname{dist}(\hat{x}_{t+1},\partial\mathcal{V}(\bar{C})) - \|x_{t+1}-\hat{x}_{t+1}\| > \epsilon > 0,

so x_{t+1} lies in the interior of \mathcal{V}(\bar{C}). \square

Proposition B.12 (Violation bound). Under Assumptions B.9–B.10, with the verification layer admitting only proposals satisfying (\hat{x}_t,u_t)\in\mathcal{K}_X and \hat{x}_{t+1}\in\mathcal{V}_\epsilon(\bar{C}), the violation frequency of the shielded system satisfies

\nu_{\text{shielded}} \;\le\; \eta,

where \eta is the false-negative rate of the verification layer on the realised state-estimate distribution.

Proof. A violation occurs at period t+1 only if x_{t+1}\notin\mathcal{K}_X. By Lemma B.11 this requires either that the verification layer admitted a proposal with \hat{x}_{t+1}\notin\mathcal{V}_\epsilon(\bar{C}) (a false negative) or that the fallback was applied and the fallback itself left \mathcal{K}_X. The second event has probability zero by the pre-verified fallback condition. Hence violation requires a false negative, whose frequency is \eta. \square

The bound is conditional on \eta, which is measured rather than assumed. The numerical protocol reports \eta under simulated noise with \epsilon\in\{0,\epsilon_{0.5},\epsilon_{0.9}\}, where \epsilon_{q} is the q-quantile of the simulated estimation error. A shield that is sound only at \epsilon=0 satisfies the lemma vacuously and is reported as such.

B.5 Proposition 4 — non-vacuity frontier

Let \Xi(\rho) denote a family of uncertainty sets parameterised by a scalar amplitude \rho (e.g., \rho times the historical standard deviation of precipitation and energy price). Define the non-vacuity frontier for installed capacity \bar{C}:

\rho^*(\bar{C}) \;=\; \sup\bigl\{\rho\ge 0 : \mathcal{V}^{\Xi(\rho)}(\bar{C})\neq\varnothing\bigr\}.

Proposition B.13 (Frontier monotonicity). If \rho_1<\rho_2 then \Xi(\rho_1)\subseteq\Xi(\rho_2), and hence \mathcal{V}^{\Xi(\rho_2)}(\bar{C})\subseteq\mathcal{V}^{\Xi(\rho_1)}(\bar{C}). Consequently \rho^*(\bar{C}) is well-defined and the kernel is empty for all \rho>\rho^*(\bar{C}).

Proof. The worst-case kernel over a larger disturbance set is the intersection of kernels over the individual disturbance trajectories; adding trajectories to the set can only remove initial states. Monotonicity follows. \square

The reported object is the surface \rho^*(\bar{C}). The framework is applicable to a given system only if \rho^*(\bar{C}) exceeds the amplitude of historically observed variability at the calibration site. If \rho^*(\bar{C}) lies below that amplitude for all \bar{C} of interest, the framework's conclusion is that the system is not robustly viable under worst-case reasoning, and the stochastic arm \mathcal{V}^{\beta} is the appropriate object.

