Appendix E — Numerical scheme for kernel computation

E.1 Reduced-state formulation

The primary kernel is computed for the reduced state

\tilde{x}_t = \bigl(\tilde{E}_t,\; L^{\mathrm{cattle}}_t,\; L^{\mathrm{small\ ruminant}}_t,\; R_t\bigr) \in \mathbb{R}^4,

where \tilde{E}_t is an aggregate ecological index formed as a weighted sum of normalised soil, water, vegetation, and nutrient indicators, with weights fixed in advance and reported. The aggregation is a modelling choice; the reduced-state kernel is a kernel of the aggregated system, not an approximation of the full-state kernel, and it is reported as such.

E.2 Hamilton–Jacobi–Isaacs formulation

The robust kernel is the zero-sublevel set of the value function V:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R} solving the constrained Hamilton–Jacobi–Isaacs equation

\min\Bigl\{0,\; H(\tilde{x},\nabla V)\Bigr\} = 0,

with Hamiltonian

H(\tilde{x},p) = \min_{u\in\mathcal{U}(\tilde{x})}\ \max_{\xi\in\Xi}\ \Bigl\{ p^\top f(\tilde{x},u,\xi) + \ell(\tilde{x},u) \Bigr\},

where \ell is the running admissibility cost (negative inside \mathcal{K}_X, positive outside). The boundary condition is V=0 on \partial\mathcal{K}_X and V>0 outside. The kernel is \{\tilde{x}: V(\tilde{x})\le 0\}.

E.3 Discretisation

The equation is solved on a uniform grid with spacing h in each dimension. Spatial derivatives are approximated by the Local Lax–Friedrichs scheme, which is monotone and converges to the viscosity solution:

\frac{V^{n+1}_i - V^n_i}{\Delta t} + H\Bigl(\tilde{x}_i,\; \nabla^- V^n_i,\; \nabla^+ V^n_i\Bigr) - \frac{c_{\mathrm{diss}}}{2}\sum_{d=1}^{4}\frac{V^n_{i+e_d} - 2V^n_i + V^n_{i-e_d}}{h} = 0,

with numerical dissipation coefficient c_{\mathrm{diss}} set to the maximum local characteristic speed. The time step satisfies the CFL condition

\Delta t \;\le\; \frac{h}{\max_{i}\bigl(\|f(\tilde{x}_i,u,\xi)\|_\infty\bigr) + 4c_{\mathrm{diss}}/h}.

E.4 Convergence check

The kernel is computed at grid spacings h and h/2. The symmetric difference volume

\Delta_h = \frac{|\mathcal{V}_h \triangle \mathcal{V}_{h/2}|}{|\mathcal{V}_{h/2}|}

is reported. A run is accepted as converged if \Delta_h < 0.05. If convergence is not achieved at the largest feasible resolution, the result is reported as unresolved, and no proposition is tested on that run.

E.5 Stochastic kernel

The stochastic kernel \mathcal{V}^{\beta} is computed by Monte Carlo over the empirical disturbance distribution. For each initial grid point, N_{\mathrm{MC}} disturbance trajectories are sampled, and the point is retained if at least a fraction \beta of the resulting trajectories remain in \mathcal{K}_X under the receding-horizon feedback. The standard error of the retained fraction is reported, and N_{\mathrm{MC}} is chosen so that the standard error is below 0.01.

