Appendix F — Inner and outer approximations for the full-state kernel

F.1 Definitions

For the full state x\in\mathbb{R}^{n_x} with n_x\ge 10, grid-based kernel computation is infeasible. Two certified approximations are reported.

An inner approximation \underline{\mathcal{V}}(\bar{C})\subseteq\mathcal{V}(\bar{C}) is a set for which membership is established by an explicit viable feedback. It is constructed by restricting attention to a parameterised family of feedbacks \{\mathfrak{u}_\theta\}_{\theta\in\Theta} and taking

\underline{\mathcal{V}}(\bar{C}) = \bigcup_{\theta\in\Theta}\ \bigl\{ x_0 : \text{the trajectory under }\mathfrak{u}_\theta\text{ remains in }\mathcal{K}_X\text{ for all }\xi\in\Xi\bigr\}.

Membership in \underline{\mathcal{V}} is certifiable by simulation of the closed loop, so the inner approximation is sound by construction.

An outer approximation \overline{\mathcal{V}}(\bar{C})\supseteq\mathcal{V}(\bar{C}) is constructed by relaxing the coupling constraint, i.e., replacing q_t^{\mathrm{proc}}\le\min\{C_t,\Phi(x_t)\} with q_t^{\mathrm{proc}}\le\Phi(x_t) and dropping the minimum-throughput requirement. The relaxed kernel is an upper bound because every viable trajectory of the coupled system is viable in the relaxed system.

F.2 Reported quantities

The reported kernel volume is |\underline{\mathcal{V}}(\bar{C})|, a lower bound on |\mathcal{V}(\bar{C})|. The gap

G(\bar{C}) = \frac{|\overline{\mathcal{V}}(\bar{C})| - |\underline{\mathcal{V}}(\bar{C})|}{|\overline{\mathcal{V}}(\bar{C})|}

is reported as a measure of approximation quality. A capacity-overhang conclusion based on the inner approximation is reported as certified if \kappa(\bar{C}) computed from \underline{\mathcal{V}} exhibits the predicted non-increase and the gap G does not exceed 0.25. If the gap exceeds that threshold, the conclusion is reported as indicative only.

F.3 Relation between reduced-state and full-state kernels

The reduced-state kernel is not claimed to approximate the full-state kernel. It is a kernel of a different dynamical system, obtained by aggregating the ecological state and merging animal categories. The relationship between the two is reported as a separate object: the projection of the full-state inner approximation onto the reduced coordinates,

\Pi\,\underline{\mathcal{V}}(\bar{C}) = \bigl\{ \tilde{x} : \exists x\in\underline{\mathcal{V}}(\bar{C}),\ \Pi x = \tilde{x}\bigr\},

and the comparison of this projection with the reduced-state kernel. Agreement between the two is evidence that the aggregation preserves the qualitative kernel structure; disagreement is evidence that it does not. No claim of approximation is made beyond this qualitative comparison.

