For an estimated welfare indicator h_j(x_t) with posterior mean \hat{h}_j and standard deviation s_j, the constraint is enforced on the lower \alpha_j-quantile:

\hat{h}_j - z_{1-\alpha_j}\,s_j \ge \underline{h}_j,

where z_{1-\alpha_j} is the standard normal quantile. This makes the admissible set well-defined under measurement noise and prevents the non-compensation principle from being silently relaxed when the indicator is estimated rather than measured. The posterior standard deviation s_j is reported per indicator, and indicators for which s_j is large enough to make the constraint unenforceable are flagged in the results.

G.3 Interaction with kernel shrinkage

The welfare quantile enforcement is a state constraint on the estimate, not on the true state. It therefore interacts with the measurement-error margin of Appendix B.4: the shrunk kernel \mathcal{V}_\epsilon is computed with respect to the quantile-enforced admissible set, and the margin \kappa(\epsilon) covers both the estimation error in the dynamics and the tail risk in the welfare constraint. The two sources of conservatism are reported separately.

