Model M0, M1, M2, M3, M4 Constraint and decision logic
Installed capacity \bar{C} 8 levels, spanning 0.25\Phi_{\max} to 2.0\Phi_{\max} Proposition 1
Minimum load \alpha 3 levels: 0.4, 0.65, 0.9 Proposition 1
Closure cost c_{\mathrm{close}} 3 levels: 0, 3, 12 months of d_1 Proposition 1, exit pathway
Herd composition 5 pre-registered compositions Proposition 2
Disturbance amplitude \rho 5 levels Proposition 4
Noise level \epsilon 2 levels Proposition 3a
Override parameters 3 (p_{\mathrm{ov}},\delta) pairs Proposition 3b

The full factorial is not run; the design is blocked. Proposition 1 uses the capacity \times load \times closure block. Proposition 2 uses the composition \times model block at fixed \bar{C}=\bar{C}^*. Proposition 3 uses the noise \times override block at fixed \bar{C} and composition. Proposition 4 uses the amplitude block at fixed \bar{C}. The blocking is pre-registered, and no cross-block interaction is tested unless the block-level results warrant it.

H.2 Replication and uncertainty

Each cell is run with R=100 disturbance replicates for the stochastic arm and with the full worst-case trajectory for the robust arm. Kernel-volume estimates are reported with bootstrap confidence intervals over the replicates. Binomial confidence intervals are reported for violation frequencies. No proposition is tested on a cell with fewer than 30 effective replicates.

H.3 Reporting of negative and null results

A null result on any proposition is reported with: the parameter regime in which it was obtained, the value of the metrics, and the pre-registered threshold that was not met. A null result at high closure cost for Proposition 1 is reported as evidence for the exit pathway, not as a failure of the framework. A null result on Proposition 2's directional condition is reported with the observed ranking direction and the channel (offtake variability or forage demand) that is consistent with it.

